course: dictionary
lesson: 5637
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning the writer means. «Picture a striker standing next to the keeper all game waiting for a long ball: the linesman's flag goes up every time, because that is offside.» The flag and offside are the clues: here a linesman is a sports official who watches the lines.
explain: Ask yourself what is happening. This sentence is about a football match, so the sport meaning fits. Then test the other meaning: a worker who fixes telephone lines does not raise a flag when a player is offside. So that meaning does not fit here.
explain: Some job names change over time. In football, linesmen are now officially called assistant referees, so you may see either name in books, on the telly or in the news.
fact: A linesman is a noun, and its plural is linesmen.
fact: In sport, a linesman helps the referee or umpire by watching the lines of the pitch or court.
fact: A linesman shows with a flag when the ball goes out or a player is offside.
fact: A linesman can also be a worker who puts up and repairs telephone, electricity or railway lines.
fact: In tennis, line judges call «Out!» when the ball lands outside the lines.
exercise: «The linesman's flag goes up when the ball goes out.» What is the linesman doing in this sentence? | *Watching the lines and showing with a flag | Cooking the team's dinner | Kicking the ball back into play | Mending telephone wires
exercise: After a storm, the power lines are down in the village. Which kind of linesman would fix them? | A referee in a football match | *A worker who repairs electricity lines | A player who is offside | A line judge in tennis
exercise: «Because that is offside, the linesman raises the flag.» What does the flag tell us? | *A player was offside | It has started to rain | The game has finished | The keeper has caught the ball
test: The linesman put up his flag. What is the most likely place for him to be? | In the school canteen | *Beside the pitch, watching the lines | Up a telegraph pole | Inside the goal net
test: Grandad says the linesmen came to fix the railway lines. Which meaning of linesman is he using? | A referee who blows a whistle | A tennis player who calls «Out!» | An official who raises a flag in sport | *A worker who repairs railway lines
exam: Ella reads «The linesman raised his flag for offside» and says it means a worker fixing lines. Which clue shows she is wrong? | The word his, because linesmen are always women | The word raised, because workers never raise anything | The word offside, because it is a kind of weather | *Raised his flag for offside, which is a sports decision
